Installing Vault
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install HashiCorp Vault on a Linux server
Configure Vault for standalone operation
Verify the installation using the Vault CLI
Understand basic Vault architecture and components
Initialize and unseal a Vault instance
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic knowledge of Linux command line operations
Understanding of file permissions and directory structures
Familiarity with text editors like nano or vim
Basic understanding of security concepts like encryption and secrets management
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided Linux machine is bare metal with no pre-installed tools, so you will need to install Vault and any required dependencies during this lab exercise.

Task 1: Install Vault on a Linux Server
Subtask 1.1: Update System Packages
First, ensure your Linux system is up to date with the latest packages.

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
Subtask 1.2: Install Required Dependencies
Install essential tools needed for the Vault installation process.

sudo apt install -y wget curl unzip gpg software-properties-common
Subtask 1.3: Add HashiCorp GPG Key
Add the official HashiCorp GPG key to verify package authenticity.

wget -O- https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg
Subtask 1.4: Add HashiCorp Repository
Add the HashiCorp repository to your system's package sources.

echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list
Subtask 1.5: Update Package List
Update the package list to include the newly added HashiCorp repository.

sudo apt update
Subtask 1.6: Install Vault
Install Vault using the package manager.

sudo apt install -y vault
Subtask 1.7: Verify Vault Binary Installation
Check if Vault binary is installed and accessible in the system PATH.

which vault
Expected output should show the path to the Vault binary, typically:

/usr/bin/vault
Subtask 1.8: Create Vault User and Directories
Create a dedicated user for running Vault and set up necessary directories.

sudo useradd --system --home /etc/vault.d --shell /bin/false vault
sudo mkdir -p /opt/vault/data
sudo mkdir -p /etc/vault.d
sudo chown -R vault:vault /opt/vault/data
sudo chown -R vault:vault /etc/vault.d
Subtask 1.9: Create Vault Configuration File
Create a basic configuration file for Vault standalone operation.

sudo tee /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl > /dev/null <<EOF
ui = true
disable_mlock = true

storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address     = "0.0.0.0:8200"
  tls_disable = 1
}

api_addr = "http://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"
EOF
Subtask 1.10: Set Proper File Permissions
Ensure the configuration file has appropriate permissions for security.

sudo chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
sudo chmod 640 /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
Subtask 1.11: Create Systemd Service File
Create a systemd service file to manage Vault as a system service.

sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/vault.service > /dev/null <<EOF
[Unit]
Description=HashiCorp Vault
Documentation=https://www.vaultproject.io/docs/
Requires=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
ConditionFileNotEmpty=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
StartLimitIntervalSec=60
StartLimitBurst=3

[Service]
Type=notify
User=vault
Group=vault
ProtectSystem=full
ProtectHome=read-only
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
SecureBits=keep-caps
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK
Capabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK+ep
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_SYSLOG CAP_IPC_LOCK
NoNewPrivileges=yes
ExecStart=/usr/bin/vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
ExecReload=/bin/kill --signal HUP \$MAINPID
KillMode=process
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
TimeoutStopSec=30
StartLimitInterval=60
StartLimitBurst=3
LimitNOFILE=65536
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF
Subtask 1.12: Enable and Start Vault Service
Enable the Vault service to start automatically on boot and start it immediately.

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable vault
sudo systemctl start vault
Subtask 1.13: Check Vault Service Status
Verify that the Vault service is running properly.

sudo systemctl status vault
Expected output should show the service as active (running).

Task 2: Verify Installation Using the Vault CLI
Subtask 2.1: Set Vault Address Environment Variable
Configure the environment variable to point to your local Vault instance.

export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
echo 'export VAULT_ADDR="http://127.0.0.1:8200"' >> ~/.bashrc
Subtask 2.2: Check Vault Status
Verify that Vault is accessible via the CLI and check its current status.

vault status
Expected output should show Vault is running but sealed and not initialized.

Subtask 2.3: Initialize Vault
Initialize Vault with default settings (5 key shares, 3 key threshold).

vault operator init
Important: Save the output containing the 5 unseal keys and initial root token in a secure location. You will need these for unsealing Vault and initial authentication.

Subtask 2.4: Unseal Vault
Use three of the five unseal keys to unseal Vault. Run the following command three times with different unseal keys:

vault operator unseal
When prompted, enter one of the unseal keys from the initialization output. Repeat this process three times with different keys.

Subtask 2.5: Authenticate with Root Token
Authenticate to Vault using the initial root token from the initialization output.

vault auth
When prompted, enter the Initial Root Token from the initialization step.

Subtask 2.6: Verify Vault Functionality
Test basic Vault functionality by checking the status again.

vault status
The output should now show:

Sealed: false
Initialized: true
Subtask 2.7: Test Secret Storage
Create a test secret to verify Vault is working correctly.

vault kv put secret/hello foo=world
Subtask 2.8: Retrieve Test Secret
Retrieve the test secret to confirm storage and retrieval functionality.

vault kv get secret/hello
Expected output should display the key-value pair you stored.

Subtask 2.9: List Available Auth Methods
Check the available authentication methods in your Vault instance.

vault auth list
Subtask 2.10: List Available Secret Engines
Display the mounted secret engines in your Vault instance.

vault secrets list
Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions
Issue: Vault service fails to start

Solution: Check the configuration file syntax using vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl -test
Issue: Permission denied errors

Solution: Verify file ownership and permissions for /opt/vault/data and /etc/vault.d/
Issue: Cannot connect to Vault

Solution: Ensure the VAULT_ADDR environment variable is set correctly and the service is running
Issue: Vault is sealed after restart

Solution: This is normal behavior. You must unseal Vault after each restart using the unseal keys
Verification Commands
To verify your installation is complete and working:

# Check service status
sudo systemctl status vault

# Verify Vault is responding
vault status

# Test authentication
vault auth list

# Check mounted secret engines
vault secrets list
Conclusion
Congratulations! You have successfully completed Lab 1: Installing Vault. In this lab, you accomplished the following:

Installed HashiCorp Vault on a Linux server using the official repository
Configured Vault for standalone operation with file storage backend
Created a systemd service to manage Vault as a system service
Initialized and unsealed a new Vault instance
Verified the installation using the Vault CLI
Tested basic functionality by storing and retrieving secrets
This installation provides you with a fully functional Vault instance running on a single Linux machine. You now have a solid foundation for exploring Vault's advanced features such as authentication methods, secret engines, policies, and more complex deployment scenarios.

Key Takeaways:

Vault requires initialization and unsealing before it can be used
The unseal keys and root token are critical for Vault operations and should be stored securely
Vault runs as a system service and can be managed using standard systemctl commands
The file storage backend is suitable for development and testing but not recommended for production use
This standalone Vault installation serves as an excellent starting point for learning secrets management, implementing security policies, and understanding how modern applications can securely store and retrieve sensitive information.
